    "abstract": "Abstract Abrupt Arctic warming has profound implications for both polar and mid-latitude climates, with moisture transport playing a central role in Arctic amplification. Using a tagged water vapor transport model driven by three reanalysis datasets, we quantify seasonal Arctic moisture transport during 1980\u20132024 and evaluate the inter-dataset spread. Robust features across all datasets indicate that summer moistening is primarily caused by increased inflow of continental moisture, while autumn moistening is dominated by local evaporation over the Arctic Ocean. Although evaporation trends exhibit substantial inter-reanalysis discrepancies, the associated summer moisture transport into the Arctic is remarkably consistent across datasets, underscoring the dominant role of circulation changes. Simulations further show that sea ice retreat induces the Arctic Dipole pattern that reinforces moisture transport and radiative feedbacks, suggesting a previously unrecognized feedback loop. Our findings highlight both the robust and uncertain components of the Arctic moisture cycle and emphasize the need to better constrain surface evaporation in Arctic climate modeling.",
    "field": "Arctic climate science",
    "problem": "The relative contributions of oceanic evaporation versus continental wetting to Arctic moistening and the role of atmospheric circulation in steering these pathways are poorly quantified, obscuring the feedback loops that link these processes to accelerated sea-ice melt and climate change.",
    "method": "We use a tagged water vapor transport model driven by three reanalysis datasets to quantify seasonal changes in Arctic moisture transport and attribute moisture origins to continental versus oceanic sources.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the seasonal sources of Arctic moisture transport from 1980-2024 using a tagged water vapor transport model to understand the links between sea-ice melting, continental wetting, and circulation changes.",
